# biggs.dog — agent routing rules

This is a Flux GitOps repo for a Kubernetes homelab (`clusters/cluster0`).
The kagent MCP server (`https://mcp.biggs.dog/mcp`) exposes specialized
agents via two tools: `list_agents` (what's actually invocable right now) and
`invoke_agent` (run a task on `ai-system/<name>`). When a task matches one of
the domains below, prefer invoking the matching kagent agent over answering
from memory or using generic tools.

> ALWAYS call `list_agents` first if you are unsure what is live. The agent set
> is mid-migration to the substrate runtime and the invocable list changes;
> the routing table below documents intent, not a guarantee that every agent is
> ready. See "Current state" before relying on an agent.

## MCP endpoint topology

The MCP endpoint is the `kagent-mcp` HTTPRoute (`ai-system`) on the internal
LAN-only gateway `internal-biggs-dog` (agentgateway, VIP `192.168.6.15`),
backed by `Service/kagent-controller:8083`. k8s-gateway split-horizon resolves
`mcp.biggs.dog` to `.6.15` for LAN/VPN clients; TLS terminates with the
wildcard cert, no auth. WAN clients hit the public gateway (no such route) and
404. Read-only inspection of the controller is also possible via
`kubectl port-forward -n ai-system svc/kagent-controller 8083:8083` (see
`.scripts/test-agents.py`).

Note: long agent runs (e.g. flux-agent ~70–75s) can exceed short client
timeouts. If `invoke_agent` times out from the editor, retry or fall back to
the port-forward path / direct `kubectl`.

## Model backend

All agents share one in-cluster vLLM backend (`vllm.ai-system:8000`,
OpenAI-compatible), served as `qwen36-local` (weights
`rdtand/Qwen3.6-27B-PrismaSCOUT-Blackwell-NVFP4-BF16-vllm`, a dense 27B model)
on `nv-01`'s RTX 5090. Inference never leaves the cluster. Served at a 131K
context window (262K native, capped via `--max-model-len=131072` for KV
concurrency headroom), fp8 KV cache, prefix caching + qwen3 tool/reasoning
parsers enabled. Throughput is solid with real concurrency for agent fan-out,
so multi-step agent tasks complete — including heavy ones like flux-agent
(whose `flux-operator-mcp` tool schemas alone are ~64K tokens). Long-term
(cross-session) vector memory is enabled (`vectorEnabled: true`); flux-agent
disables per-agent memory to stay under the window. Prefer terse output.

## Architecture: two agent runtimes

1. **Declarative agents (python runtime).** Plain `Agent` CRs
   (`kubectl get agents -n ai-system`). Each runs as its own Deployment and is
   directly invocable. This is the stable path.
2. **Substrate SandboxAgents.** The agent substrate (kagent ATE / actor
   runtime) is deployed in `apps/ai-system/substrate/` (+ `substrate-crds/`),
   with worker `atelet` pods, `ate-controller`, `ate-api-server`, and
   `atenet-router`. Five agents have been re-declared as `SandboxAgent` CRs
   (`kubectl get sandboxagents -n ai-system`) in
   `apps/ai-system/kagent/substrate-agents/` and disabled in the chart so the
   chart can't also render them as python Agents. SandboxAgents support
   sandboxed code execution.

## Current state (verify before relying on it)

- **Invocable now (Declarative, stable):**
  `flux-agent`, `vm-agent`, `exa-agent`, `cilium-debug-agent`,
  `cilium-policy-agent`.
- **Substrate SandboxAgents: READY=True, but NOT in the MCP `list_agents`
  output (so `invoke_agent` returns "not found").** `k8s-agent`, `helm-agent`,
  `promql-agent`, `observability-agent`, `cilium-manager-agent` are
  `ACCEPTED=True` / `READY=True` (golden actors built; ActorTemplates in
  `Ready` phase, zero reconcile errors; upserted into the kagent Postgres
  `agents` table on reconcile). The kagent HTTP REST API (`/agents`, kagent-ui)
  DOES list them; only the MCP `list_agents` tool omits them. Keep using the
  fallbacks below / direct `kubectl` until this is fixed.

  Root cause of the MCP listing gap — an **upstream kagent limitation, NOT a
  cluster config issue**, and NOT fixed on `kagent-dev/kagent@main` as of this
  writing. `MCPHandler.listReadyAgents` (go/core/internal/mcp/mcp_handler.go)
  (a) lists only `v1alpha2.AgentList` — it never enumerates `SandboxAgent`
  CRs at all, and (b) hard-codes `condition.Reason == "DeploymentReady"`.
  Declarative Agents report Ready with reason `DeploymentReady`; substrate
  SandboxAgents report Ready with reason `WorkloadReady`. So substrate agents
  are excluded twice (wrong CR type + wrong reason). Fix needs a kagent change
  (also list `SandboxAgentList` and accept `WorkloadReady`); track upstream and
  bump kagent when fixed. (`invoke_agent` is ALSO blocked by the same gap —
  verified: `invoke_agent ai-system/k8s-agent` returns "agent not found or not
  ready" — so substrate agents are not usable via the MCP layer in 0.9.10.)

  Root cause of the long-running degradation, and what's fixed (all verified
  live on `main`). This cluster runs substrate in `ai-system`, but upstream
  hard-codes the `ate-system` namespace everywhere, so a stack of
  `ate-system`-vs-`ai-system` bugs gated golden-actor creation, each hidden
  behind the previous — and the final blocker was a Cilium Network Policy gap,
  NOT "no pod NAT" as earlier inferred:
    1. ate-controller dial addr (`--ateapi-conn-spec`, substrate HelmRelease).
    2. ate-api-server JWT key fetch (`--client-jwt-jwks-url`, forked image
       `docker.io/shrinedogg/ateapi` — see `.wip/substrate-jwks-url/`).
    3. ate-api-server atelet discovery (`ATELET_NAMESPACE=ai-system` on the
       forked image — see `.wip/substrate-atelet-namespace/`).
    4. kagent env-source RBAC (`controller.substrate.ateApiServer.namespace=`
       `ai-system` in the kagent HelmRelease — the RoleBinding subject was
       pointed at the nonexistent `ate-system` SA).
    5. gVisor `runsc` asset (`SandboxConfig/gvisor-default` + `runsc-cache`
       DaemonSet; runsc 20260622.0 staged in rustfs `ate-snapshots` and
       pre-cached per node — the atelet's S3 asset client doesn't honor
       `AWS_ENDPOINT_URL`).
    6. **Cilium egress (the real final blocker):** ai-system is in Cilium
       default-deny, and `allow-egress-internet` selected only
       `managed-by=kagent` pods. The atelet (`app=atelet`, Helm-managed)
       pulls sandbox container images in-process (`memorypullcache`, does NOT
       reuse node containerd) from `cr.kagent.dev` + `gcr.io`, so its pulls
       were policy-denied (`dial tcp ...:443: i/o timeout`; hostNetwork bypassed
       it because Cilium enforces on pod identity). Fixed by
       `allow-egress-atelet-registries` (CNP, `app=atelet` -> `world:443`).
  Notes: node containerd pulls (for ordinary Deployments) always worked (host
  network); only the atelet's pod-network in-process pulls were blocked. There
  IS selective pod-network egress to `world:443` via CNP for labeled pods
  (kagent agents, vllm, embeddings) — not "no pod NAT".

To check live: `list_agents`, plus
`kubectl get agents,sandboxagents -n ai-system`, and
`kubectl logs -n ai-system deploy/ate-controller | grep -i reconciler`.

## Where agents are defined

- Declarative chart-managed Agents — kagent `HelmRelease`
  (`apps/ai-system/kagent/app/helmrelease.yaml`), toggled via per-agent
  `*.enabled` values. Flux owns these CRs; disable there, not via
  `kubectl delete`. (`argo-rollouts-agent`, `istio-agent`, `kgateway-agent`
  are disabled and not deployed.)
- `flux-agent` — `apps/ai-system/flux-mcp/` (custom, read-only Flux).
- `vm-agent` — `apps/ai-system/victoria-metrics-mcp/` (VictoriaMetrics MCP).
- `exa-agent` — `apps/ai-system/exa-mcp/` (Exa web search/research).
- `cilium-debug-agent`, `cilium-policy-agent` —
  `apps/ai-system/kagent/cilium-agents/` (chart versions disabled, re-defined
  statically so they carry generic `k8s_*` tools alongside Cilium tools).
- `k8s-agent`, `helm-agent`, `promql-agent`, `observability-agent`,
  `cilium-manager-agent` — `apps/ai-system/kagent/substrate-agents/`
  (SandboxAgents; currently degraded — see above).

Keep this list in sync with the live set.

## Agent selection

Pick the single most specific agent for the task. ✅ = invocable now,
⚠️ = substrate, currently degraded (use the fallback).

- ✅ **flux-agent** — Flux CD / GitOps inspection via the Flux Operator MCP:
  status of `FluxInstance`, sources (`GitRepository`/`OCIRepository`/
  `HelmRepository`), `Kustomization`s, `HelmRelease`s, `ResourceSet`s, plus
  end-to-end reconciliation root-cause analysis and Flux docs. Read-only. Use
  for "why isn't this reconciling / syncing from git". While `helm-agent` is
  down, this is also the best agent for live `HelmRelease` reconciliation
  state.

- ✅ **vm-agent** — Direct VictoriaMetrics: execute PromQL/MetricsQL, discover
  metrics/labels/series, inspect alerting/recording rules, analyze TSDB
  cardinality, find unused/high-cardinality metrics, diagnose slow queries.
  Backed by embedded VM docs. Use for raw metric questions and VM diagnostics.
  While `observability-agent`/`promql-agent` are down, use vm-agent for metric
  queries and PromQL authoring too.

- ✅ **exa-agent** — Web search, code discovery, company/competitor research,
  technical-docs discovery, web intelligence. Requires an Exa API key.

- ✅ **cilium-debug-agent** — Diagnosing a broken/degraded Cilium install:
  connectivity failures, dropped traffic, advanced diagnostics, Hubble flow
  inspection. Carries generic `k8s_*` tools, so while `k8s-agent` is down it
  doubles as a general cluster-inspection agent.

- ✅ **cilium-policy-agent** — Authoring `CiliumNetworkPolicy` /
  `CiliumClusterwideNetworkPolicy` from natural-language intent. Use only when
  the deliverable is a policy manifest. Also carries generic `k8s_*` tools.

- ⚠️ **k8s-agent** — General Kubernetes ops, resource inspection, and
  troubleshooting (pods, deployments, services, events, nodes). Default for
  "what's wrong in the cluster". **Fallback while degraded:** use
  `cilium-debug-agent` (has `k8s_*` tools) or direct `kubectl`.

- ⚠️ **observability-agent** — Monitoring/observability: querying metrics,
  building/verifying Grafana dashboards (grafana-mcp, ~65 tools), checking
  alerting, correlating cluster state with metrics. **Fallback while
  degraded:** `vm-agent` for metrics; Grafana dashboard work must wait for the
  actor to recover.

- ⚠️ **promql-agent** — Generating a PromQL query from a natural-language
  description. **Fallback while degraded:** `vm-agent`.

- ⚠️ **helm-agent** — Helm release management/troubleshooting (install,
  upgrade, rollback, values, failed releases). This repo drives Helm via Flux
  `HelmRelease`, so edit the `HelmRelease` YAML for desired state regardless.
  **Fallback while degraded:** `flux-agent` for live release state, plus direct
  `helm`/`kubectl`.

- ⚠️ **cilium-manager-agent** — Installing, configuring, upgrading, and
  routine monitoring of Cilium ("set up / change Cilium"). **Fallback while
  degraded:** `cilium-debug-agent` for inspection; make config changes by
  editing manifests in the repo.

## Repo conventions

- Desired state lives in Git (`clusters/cluster0/**`); Flux reconciles it. Use
  agents to inspect/diagnose *live* state, but make persistent changes by
  editing the YAML here, not by mutating the cluster directly.
- Secrets are managed via External Secrets + 1Password; never hardcode
  credentials in manifests. (Where 1Password items don't yet exist, a
  temporary in-cluster Secret + a post-renderer env patch is used — e.g.
  `kagent-grafana-mcp-token`; replace with an ExternalSecret once the item
  exists.)
- The branch Flux tracks is `main`. Pushing fixes to a feature branch will not
  reconcile — merge to `main`.
